WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.735 --> 00:00:05.110
MATTHEW MEHAN:
Welcome, everyone,

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:08.320
to the Allan P. Kirby Junior
Center for Constitutional

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.530
Studies and Citizenship.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.660
And it's also the location
of Hillsdale and DC's

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.020
newly launched Steve and Amy
Van Andel Graduate School

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.900
of Government.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.500
My name is Dr. Matthew Mehan.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.510
I am the director
of academic programs

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.260
here at the Kirby Center.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.070
And I teach both
the undergraduates

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.430
who come visit from the main
campus in Hillsdale, Michigan

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.890
and also in the grad
school, as well as

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.650
other more bureaucratic duties.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.860
But I also get the
privilege of conducting

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.420
some of these book interviews.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:47.370
So before I go any
further, I want

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:50.850
to introduce Richard K. Vedder.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.910
He's a senior fellow at
the Independent Institute,

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.400
a distinguished emeritus
professor of economics

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.290
at Ohio University.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.740
And I just met a
number of his students

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.090
who came all the way
out from there tonight.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.090
So that's a great
honor for a teacher,

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:09.510
to have loyal students
who will travel.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.220
That's great.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.960
He has been senior economist at
the US Joint Economic Committee

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.510
and visiting fellow at
the Center for the Study

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:22.320
of American Business,
Washington University--

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.570
in my hometown, St. Louis.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.730
Besides his most
recent book, Dr. Vedder

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:30.180
is also the author of
Going Broke by Degree,

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.490
Why College Costs Too Much.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.310
His articles have appeared in
numerous scholarly journals,

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.830
The Wall Street Journal,
Christian Science Monitor,

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.740
National Review, Washington
Times, and Investor's Business

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.270
Daily.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.700
And relevant to our purpose
tonight, and perhaps

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.460
also his most
impressive accolade,

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:52.690
Professor Vedder has been
a teacher for 55 years.

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:57.330
RICHARD VEDDER: Oh, 54
years, 55 years, something--

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:58.140
I lose count.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.910
At my age, I can't
count very well.

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:02.590
But it's something
like that, yes.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.310
MATTHEW MEHAN: Wise
and experienced.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.590
He also has deep
roots in Illinois.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.990
That makes him a
Midwesterner, thereby

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:12.390
earning him the title
to be held hereafter

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.973
in perpetuity of Good People.

00:02:13.973 --> 00:02:15.390
RICHARD VEDDER:
Yes, that's right.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.515
MATTHEW MEHAN: That's good.

00:02:16.515 --> 00:02:19.260
I'm from St. Louis, my wife's
from Springfield, Illinois.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.120
And for you Gen
Xers in the crowd,

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.710
I regret to inform
you that he is,

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.480
I believe, no relation
to Eddie Vedder,

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.275
lead singer of Pearl Jam.

00:02:25.275 --> 00:02:26.608
RICHARD VEDDER: Absolutely none.

00:02:26.608 --> 00:02:27.780
Never met the guy.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.910
MATTHEW MEHAN: Even
though Eddie Vedder

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.438
is from Evanston, Illinois.

00:02:32.438 --> 00:02:34.230
RICHARD VEDDER: The
school that I attended,

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.640
actually, is in that town.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.760
MATTHEW MEHAN: And I will try
to use some references to lyrics

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.700
to Pearl Jam throughout
the interview.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.890
But I'd like to thank
you, Dr. Vedder,

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:50.760
for writing this book,
Restoring the Promise, Higher

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.110
Education in America.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:56.550
The Kirby Center here in DC, and
Hillsdale College in general,

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:59.580
we care a lot about
trying to build up

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:04.130
and enrich constitutional
self-government in our country.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:06.270
And I was just teaching
the grad program--

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.880
the other day, we were going
through Aristotle's politics,

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.370
wherein Aristotle just
drops this bomb, oh,

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:15.630
by the way, if you actually care
about politics, statesmanship,

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.850
and leadership, your
fundamental concern

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:22.420
would obviously be the education
that perpetuates the regime.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.537
It's just a startling drop.

00:03:25.537 --> 00:03:27.120
And you've applied
much of your career

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.260
and now this book in particular
to taking up higher education,

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.990
where future educators
and future leaders

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:38.370
in pretty much all fields
are formed and informed.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:40.740
That is to say, I'm very
happy to have this discussion

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.380
with you, especially
about this subject

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:47.325
because it's fundamental to
our concerns of the common good

00:03:47.325 --> 00:03:50.400
and to the entire country
and to self-government.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.530
So without any further
ado, and throat clearing,

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.740
I just want to ask you
to give us the bird's eye

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:03.330
view, the elevator pitch,
what is Restoring the Promise,

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:04.590
Higher Education in America?

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:05.633
What's the promise?

00:04:05.633 --> 00:04:07.050
RICHARD VEDDER:
Well, I don't know

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:10.830
what the promise is because
I didn't pick the title.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:15.090
Some marketing guru
picked the title,

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:18.810
and darned if I know
what the promise is.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:20.289
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.270
However, having
said that, I think

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:28.710
the promise relates to the
fact that higher education is

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:33.120
to serve the common good
and is to serve society

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.160
in a very positive way.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:40.020
And to some extent, it does
that, but in some extent,

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:41.350
it fails.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:45.960
And so how can we make it
come closer to the ideal?

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:53.070
And I suspect the restoring
the promise refers to that.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:56.130
But the reason I
wrote the book is

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:59.850
that there are several
problems with higher

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.490
education in America.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:06.510
All of you know what they are.

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:08.730
I'll use my fingers here.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:12.190
We don't have PowerPoint
here, which is--

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:13.770
I'm grateful for
that, by the way.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:15.460
MATTHEW MEHAN: You and I
share an abiding hatred

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:15.750
for PowerPoint.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.167
RICHARD VEDDER: I
hate PowerPoint.

00:05:17.167 --> 00:05:20.480
I'll use what I call, and I
hope this doesn't offend anyone,

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.130
West Virginia PowerPoint.

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:23.793
I'll use my fingers.

00:05:23.793 --> 00:05:25.376
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:28.474 --> 00:05:30.516
MATTHEW MEHAN: He does
not speak for the college.

00:05:30.516 --> 00:05:31.945
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:31.945 --> 00:05:33.320
RICHARD VEDDER:
Hillsdale College

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:34.690
has already disowned you.

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:37.850
Dr. Vedder, do you
want to leave now?

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:38.360
No.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.030
First, college is too expensive.

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:42.230
Everyone agrees with that.

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:44.630
There isn't a person
in America who

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:46.760
seriously disagrees with that.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:48.980
Some people--
apologists for higher ed

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:51.360
will say, it's still
a good bargain,

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.220
you're still getting
your money's worth.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:57.050
But the reality is the costs
in higher ed, however measured,

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:00.320
have risen vastly faster
than the rate of inflation,

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:03.560
et cetera for many,
many years, and I

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:04.950
can go into that in a minute.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:09.890
But let's just do the short
and dirty version of this.

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.080
Now, the second thing
that has happened,

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.750
and this is more
scandalous and I

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:18.440
hesitate to say this in
front of my own students,

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:21.950
but let's face it,
people aren't learning

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:25.280
as much as they used to and
they're not learning enough,

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:29.600
and maybe they're
learning the wrong things.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.230
There's all sorts of empirical
evidence, and some of it's

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:37.370
a little snippy, a
little limited because--

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:41.480
it's funny, in higher ed, we
are in the knowledge business.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:46.070
We like to report every little
facet of every human endeavor.

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:49.700
But the one thing we're
a little neglectful on

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:53.060
is, do we measure whether our
kids are learning anything?

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:57.260
Are they doing better now than
they did a generation ago?

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:59.320
Do seniors know
more than freshmen?

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:01.820
Do the graduates of
Hillsdale College

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:04.340
learn more in their
four years at Hillsdale

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:05.650
than the graduates of Yale?

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:09.230
There's a Yale student in the
audience, I'll pick on Yale.

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:14.100
And my guess is the answer
is yes, but I can't prove it.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.570
So there is an issue.

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:20.040
And with that is the whole
issue of suppression of ideas,

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:22.910
the lack of intellectual
diversity in universities,

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:23.700
and so forth.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:25.715
So that's the second
area of problems.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:30.725
Somewhere, I think in my--

00:07:30.725 --> 00:07:33.470
I have a great quote
from, of all people,

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:42.920
Thomas Edison, who in 1921 told
The New York Times that, look,

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.400
I hire a lot of
college graduates,

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:48.480
and I think they're
abysmally ignorant.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.040
That was in 1921.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:53.830
Fast forward 100
years, and I think

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:57.250
they know less than the
people in 1921 knew.

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:58.870
So where are we?

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:02.740
The third problem-- and
then I'll keep quiet--

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:06.910
is, are the students
today, who are

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:11.320
spending $25,000, $50,000,
$100,000, $200,000 going

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:12.010
to college--

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:16.180
and some of them go to yuppie
private proprietary schools,

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:18.810
such as one that you teach at.

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.100
I'm picking on you.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:23.207
MATTHEW MEHAN: Just a
huge field of yuppies.

00:08:23.207 --> 00:08:24.665
RICHARD VEDDER: A
world of yuppies.

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:31.570
And do they get good
jobs after they graduate?

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:33.159
Some of them do.

00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:35.350
Some of them do very well.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:37.809
My students are all
uniformly brilliant

00:08:37.809 --> 00:08:41.559
and they're all going to end up
making tons of money someday,

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:46.630
or if not tons of money,
tons of satisfaction.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:47.530
That's not true.

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:51.160
Just look at them.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:51.790
You can see.

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:58.160
But they're going to
do reasonably well.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:02.860
But the reality is a lot
of kids go to college today

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:04.780
and they don't graduate.

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:08.770
Not at Hillsdale, where 90 some
percent of the kids graduate.

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:11.050
But at schools
around the country,

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:13.300
40% of the kids
who enter college

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:16.030
do not have degrees
six years later.

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:19.490
Not four years later, not five
years later, six years later,

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:21.320
they don't have college degrees.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:22.960
So you lose 40%.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:26.200
And then many of
the others who do

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:30.670
graduate are living in
their parents' basement.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:32.500
So there's three problems.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:34.150
And there are others
we can talk about,

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:35.317
but I've talked long enough.

00:09:35.317 --> 00:09:35.940
Sorry.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:36.690
MATTHEW MEHAN: No.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:38.260
Yeah.

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:40.330
I wonder if we just
take each of these one

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:42.700
at a time for a little while.

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.280
RICHARD VEDDER: OK.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:47.020
MATTHEW MEHAN: And when we do--

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:55.360
what I most want to know
is, who do I get to blame?

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:57.700
Is it the higher ed's problem?

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:01.210
Is it a societal change?

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:03.135
Is it a governmental change?

00:10:03.135 --> 00:10:04.010
RICHARD VEDDER: Sure.

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:08.080
MATTHEW MEHAN: But
with regard to cost,

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.548
where do we point the
finger to find the problem?

00:10:10.548 --> 00:10:12.090
RICHARD VEDDER: It's
a good question.

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:19.646
I assign a large
percentage of the blame

00:10:19.646 --> 00:10:25.490
to, horrors of horrors,
the federal government,

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:28.910
the government of the
United States of America.

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:33.590
And to think that I'm saying
this within a stone's throw--

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:36.350
or a long stone's throw--

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:39.390
three blocks, four blocks--

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:41.690
at a younger age, I
could have thrown a stone

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:44.860
and hit the US
Capital from here.

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:45.360
But--

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:47.160
MATTHEW MEHAN: Again, he does
not speak for the college.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.100
RICHARD VEDDER: Yeah.

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:49.980
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:54.950
A large part of the problem
is a federal problem.

00:10:54.950 --> 00:11:00.620
And I hear it's just
as simple as can be.

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:06.400
The federal government made
a nice decision, seemingly--

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:09.340
seemingly-- it wasn't
a nice decision--

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:14.770
50 years ago, we want to
increase access to higher ed.

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:18.340
We want to help low income
people go to college.

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:20.350
Sounds like a nice thing.

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:26.500
Well, 50 years ago, 10% of
recent college graduates

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:29.490
came from the bottom quarter
of the income distribution.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:30.670
Let's call them poor people.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:33.190
10%-- no, 12%.

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:34.420
Sorry, 12%.

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:37.600
I shouldn't have had that
glass of wine before I talked.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:39.340
Now it's down to 10%--

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:40.570
it's down to 10%.

00:11:40.570 --> 00:11:42.400
It's less today.

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:47.480
Higher ed is still largely for
middle class and upper class

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:47.980
people.

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:50.750
Not entirely, but still largely.

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:54.810
But we put this in
to help poor people.

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:56.270
What did it do?

00:11:56.270 --> 00:11:56.960
What did it do?

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:59.680
What did the colleges
do when they said,

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:06.040
oh, kids can borrow money up
to the cost of attendance?

00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:10.540
So what did the colleges do?

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:14.710
They started raising their
fees aggressively, except--

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:16.990
and I'm really
serious about this--

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:20.720
except Hillsdale College
and a few others, a very,

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:23.760
very, very, very few others.

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:28.140
So here's a little factoid.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:37.770
Before 1978, going back to 1840,
138 years of American history,

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:41.670
the price of college
rose 1% a year,

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:44.310
more than the overall
inflation rate.

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:47.600
And that might be explained
by some of the arguments

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:50.850
that people use today, that
it's a service industry,

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:53.820
it's hard to substitute
professors in a classroom

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:56.670
with machines, those
kind of arguments.

00:12:56.670 --> 00:13:00.060
And a gentleman in the audience
is shaking his head no,

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:02.030
and he's right about that, too.

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:05.490
But for the moment, let's
just buy that argument.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:07.980
That might explain a little
because as the economy

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:10.980
of the whole nation
got better and richer,

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:13.080
we had to pay professors more.

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:15.330
And even though they weren't
teaching anymore students

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:17.122
than before, we still
had to pay them more.

00:13:17.122 --> 00:13:20.010
So the per student
cost rose a little.

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:22.560
Maybe that would explain the 1%.

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:27.330
Since 1978, fees have
gone up 3% a year.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:29.520
Not 1% a year, 3% a year.

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:31.390
You say, no big deal.

00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:33.480
Do a compounding.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:37.560
3% a year compounded
doubles every 24 years.

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:41.160
Over a course of less than
half a century, it quadruples.

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:46.110
Tuition fees have tripled,
adjusting for inflation,

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:47.790
since the late '70s.

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:52.020
A large part of that is due
to the government involvement.

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:53.840
Bill Bennett said it perfectly.

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:58.120
He was the former
Secretary of Education.

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:02.490
By the way, a philosophy
graduate of Williams College,

00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:06.840
trained in classical training.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:12.250
Bill Bennett said this in 1978
in a New York Times op ed,

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:14.080
and it's true today.

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:17.430
So no great revelation.

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:21.390
So the student loan program
and the federal government

00:14:21.390 --> 00:14:25.800
generally-- and I could
elaborate on this.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:29.460
I could tell you rich
stories about the Department

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:32.970
of Education and its creation.

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:34.260
Let me tell real quick.

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:37.910
This is just too
good to pass up.

00:14:37.910 --> 00:14:42.030
In 1976, Jimmy
Carter was elected

00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:45.390
president of the United States.

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:48.865
He promised a
Department of Education.

00:14:48.865 --> 00:14:51.530
Well, why did he promise
a Department of Education?

00:14:51.530 --> 00:14:54.750
Was anyone clamoring for
a Department of Education?

00:14:54.750 --> 00:15:00.510
Yes, the National
Education Association, NEA,

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:02.770
the teacher's unions.

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:04.710
He didn't do anything
for two years.

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:07.920
Jimmy is a little
slow sometimes.

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:09.090
Nice man, by the way.

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:10.470
I met Jimmy Carter.

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:13.980
My wife and I had drinks
and ate with Jimmy Carter.

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:17.400
We try to eat dinner with all
kinds of different people,

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:19.130
and Jimmy was one of them.

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:22.380
In '79, Jimmy Carter--

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:26.370
Hamilton Jordan, his chief
of staff, said, Jimmy--

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:29.310
President Carter,
probably-- President Carter,

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:34.310
you have to deliver on a
Department of Education.

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:37.130
And Carter said, why?

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:41.030
Well, the NEA wants it.

00:15:41.030 --> 00:15:42.680
OK.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:45.570
What was the reaction
of the general public

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:48.260
when Carter said I'm going for
the Department of Education?

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:51.960
What did The
Washington Post say?

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:53.250
It's a dumb idea.

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:55.500
The Washington Post!

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:57.690
What did The New York Times say?

00:15:57.690 --> 00:15:59.100
We're against it.

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:02.470
The New York Times
was against it.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:05.310
What did the most
respected intellectual

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:08.280
of the era on the
progressive side say?

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:11.400
Daniel Patrick Moynihan.

00:16:11.400 --> 00:16:12.360
It's a dumb idea.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:17.490
It passed in the House
of Representatives

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:20.280
in the Education
Committee by three votes.

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:26.130
Barely made it through Congress,
but they approved it anyway.

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:31.330
Narrowly got through
and became law.

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:33.480
What's the department
done since?

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:37.470
Is higher ed better off
than it was 50 years ago?

00:16:37.470 --> 00:16:41.160
Is public education-- K
through 12 education better off

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:43.950
than it was 50 years ago?

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:47.130
Are we as a nation
have progressed

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:51.630
in this four decades?

00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:55.650
I look at the data, I
look at the evidence--

00:16:55.650 --> 00:16:59.040
and by the way, I've been
on a public school board--

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:03.360
an elected public official on
public school boards at the K

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:04.140
through 12 level.

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:08.160
I've looked at it
throughout the whole thing.

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:11.640
I don't see an iota
of improvement.

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:14.640
And I see, in some
ways, we're worse off.

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:17.910
We're paying more,
we're getting less,

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:22.680
and we're being directed by
bureaucrats and educrats who

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:26.819
live here in Washington
DC, when sometimes,

00:17:26.819 --> 00:17:34.050
us people out in the boondocks
of wherever, flyover country--

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:37.290
I'm from flyover
country, I profess.

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:40.170
I'm not quite as smart
as some of you people

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:43.230
who live on the
east coast, where

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:45.762
knowledge is much greater,
or on the west coast,

00:17:45.762 --> 00:17:46.470
where knowledge--

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:47.051
MATTHEW MEHAN: Beware
of the dsssssssssssssss

00:17:47.051 --> 00:17:47.133
ssssssssssssssss
sssssssddddddddd

00:17:47.133 --> 00:17:47.215
dddddddddddddddd
dddddddddddddddd

00:17:47.215 --> 00:17:47.297
dddddddddddddddd
dddddddddddddddd

00:17:47.297 --> 00:17:47.380
dddddddddddddddd
ddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:47.880
speaker.

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:51.180
RICHARD VEDDER: I'm part
of the dumb middle part

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:52.125
of the country.

00:17:52.125 --> 00:17:54.750
But some of those people in the
dumb middle part of the country

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:57.760
aren't quite as dumb as
you make it out to be.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:03.500
And I think we're
doing all right, then.

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:04.540
Anyway, I'm sorry.

00:18:04.540 --> 00:18:04.830
MATTHEW MEHAN: No.

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:06.210
RICHARD VEDDER: I'm
talking too much here.

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:07.740
MATTHEW MEHAN: But
it's a question

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:09.750
I have with regard to costs.

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:14.710
So the occasion
for this ramp up--

00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:20.977
this exponential ramp up is the
federally-backed loans, right?

00:18:20.977 --> 00:18:21.810
RICHARD VEDDER: Yes.

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:24.480
MATTHEW MEHAN: But don't
we still fault higher ed

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:29.950
for greedily grabbing at it
and saddling all these students

00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:30.450
with--

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:33.370
RICHARD VEDDER: Well, the higher
ed of course grabbed at it.

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:36.000
MATTHEW MEHAN: And what
about the families?

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:37.320
RICHARD VEDDER: Oh, yeah.

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:39.760
The families be damned.

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:43.110
Well, more or less.

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:47.130
And I will say there
are exceptions.

00:18:47.130 --> 00:18:51.900
We're in the temples of
exceptions here at Hillsdale.

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:55.290
But Grove City College--
there are some other colleges

00:18:55.290 --> 00:18:58.920
that have done similar
things and have said, just

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:01.710
say no to the federal programs.

00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:05.640
So the federal programs
have been the basis for it.

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:07.290
A lot of colleges do it.

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:11.040
The people who run colleges,
they're decent people.

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:12.960
They're not all mean people.

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:15.345
They don't all beat
their wives or whatever.

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:23.970
They're morally-- they like to
say they're a little bit better

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:24.810
than the rest of us.

00:19:24.810 --> 00:19:26.685
I don't know whether
they're better or worse,

00:19:26.685 --> 00:19:28.080
but they're normal people.

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:32.280
And they're tempted--
they can say, hey,

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:36.700
we can raise our fees 6% a
year instead of 4% a year.

00:19:36.700 --> 00:19:40.350
And if we do that, we can lower
teaching loads for the faculty.

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:43.680
If we do that, we can hire
42 more administrators

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:47.340
and we can get rid
of these people

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:50.730
who are yelling and screaming
about diversity and inclusion

00:19:50.730 --> 00:19:52.650
who are giving us a hard time--

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:56.490
we can hire 42 more
diversity coordinators.

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:58.260
How many diversity
coordinators are there

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:00.090
at the University of Michigan?

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:01.230
How many?

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:04.990
Last I counted 93.

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:06.130
93.

00:20:06.130 --> 00:20:10.690
27 of them making more
than $100,000 a year.

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:12.310
You're shocked.

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:12.910
I'm not.

00:20:12.910 --> 00:20:14.050
I live in higher ed.

00:20:14.050 --> 00:20:15.230
I see it all the time.

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:16.910
And I'm not picking
just on that field.

00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:20.560
You do the same thing with
public relations specialists.

00:20:20.560 --> 00:20:24.280
You do the same thing
with athletic programs.

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:27.550
You do the same thing
across the board.

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:30.070
And it is an outrage.

00:20:30.070 --> 00:20:33.970
My university, which is a
typical state university,

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:38.230
in the mid '70s, had
two faculty members

00:20:38.230 --> 00:20:41.230
for every one administrator.

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:47.500
Now we have for every two
faculty members, 2 and 1/2

00:20:47.500 --> 00:20:48.340
administrators.

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:50.158
More administrators and faculty.

00:20:50.158 --> 00:20:52.450
In fact, I proposed to the
president of the university,

00:20:52.450 --> 00:20:56.830
why don't you give every faculty
member their own administrator?

00:20:56.830 --> 00:20:58.210
Reorganize the university.

00:20:58.210 --> 00:21:00.340
We'll give every
faculty member a valet--

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:01.810
an administrative valet.

00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:06.130
I think it'd be a cool idea.

00:21:06.130 --> 00:21:06.895
Sorry.

00:21:06.895 --> 00:21:08.020
MATTHEW MEHAN: That's good.

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:16.420
So is the proposed solution
for the federal loan program

00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:22.600
do what Hillsdale has done,
get off of the crystal meth

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:23.980
that's driving everything?

00:21:23.980 --> 00:21:25.150
RICHARD VEDDER: Yeah.

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:26.170
MATTHEW MEHAN: Or
are you proposing

00:21:26.170 --> 00:21:27.880
get rid of the
legislation altogether,

00:21:27.880 --> 00:21:30.453
that it has no public utility?

00:21:30.453 --> 00:21:31.870
RICHARD VEDDER:
Well, I would love

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:35.600
to get rid of the
legislation, I really would.

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:38.930
But I used to work
for the Gang of 535.

00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:40.890
It's across the street, roughly.

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:42.850
That is to say the US Congress.

00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:44.230
I've worked there.

00:21:44.230 --> 00:21:46.390
I know those people.

00:21:46.390 --> 00:21:49.860
I've lived with those people.

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:52.890
Have I said enough?

00:21:52.890 --> 00:21:55.980
They're not going to do
it, and I don't blame them.

00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:58.690
From a job security
point of view,

00:21:58.690 --> 00:22:01.170
most Americans think
it's a cool program,

00:22:01.170 --> 00:22:04.860
and you don't want to get
rid of something that's cool.

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:07.450
So what is the solution?

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:10.890
Well, one group of Americans--

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:13.170
and I don't want to
tie it specifically

00:22:13.170 --> 00:22:15.960
to political parties, but
in this current environment,

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:19.950
we see it especially among
progressive Democrats,

00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:24.360
Elizabeth Warren,
Bernie Sanders.

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:26.010
But now pretty much
all of them say,

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:28.760
let's just forgive the debt.

00:22:28.760 --> 00:22:32.070
Why don't we just
say, forgive the debt?

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:32.730
What the heck?

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:38.100
We just let a--

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:41.430
what's another trillion
dollars or $2 trillion?

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:48.090
We're running, by the way,
every day $3 billion deficit,

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:50.070
the federal
government every day.

00:22:50.070 --> 00:22:55.350
Do the math. $125 million an
hour, $2 million a minute.

00:22:55.350 --> 00:22:57.930
Do the math further. $30
some thousand a second.

00:22:57.930 --> 00:23:00.780
In the time it took me
to say that, we ran up

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:02.610
another half million
dollars of debt.

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:08.360
And here we are in
this environment

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:10.870
of total political
irresponsibility

00:23:10.870 --> 00:23:15.250
in both parties, Republican,
Democrat, vegetarian,

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:21.210
Presbyterian, whatever
political party you belong to,

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:23.370
and we're talking
about forgiving

00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:25.260
a trillion dollars
worth of debt--

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:27.000
a trillion and a half dollars.

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:29.090
We're going to
tell people who've

00:23:29.090 --> 00:23:32.250
worked hard their lives
paying off that debt,

00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:35.550
we're going to say,
yo, you were suckers.

00:23:35.550 --> 00:23:37.420
You were suckers.

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:41.886
Haha, you actually believed
the government, didn't you?

00:23:41.886 --> 00:23:43.620
Tough.

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:47.130
But the other people
who bought second homes

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:51.330
or took out big mortgages
and took trips to Europe

00:23:51.330 --> 00:23:56.200
and so forth didn't
pay down their debt,

00:23:56.200 --> 00:23:57.970
you don't owe anymore.

00:23:57.970 --> 00:24:00.830
Free at last.

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:03.040
I think that shameful.

00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:05.460
What do you think that
does to future obligations?

00:24:05.460 --> 00:24:08.100
What do you think it
tells Americans in terms

00:24:08.100 --> 00:24:09.450
of meeting their obligation?

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:13.380
What does it say about meeting
the sanctity of contracts?

00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:15.910
What does it say
about the rule of law?

00:24:15.910 --> 00:24:17.290
It is horrible.

00:24:17.290 --> 00:24:20.290
It's horrible,
horrible, horrible.

00:24:20.290 --> 00:24:22.340
I think I've made
that clear, didn't I?

00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:23.640
So I'll be quiet.

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:26.580
MATTHEW MEHAN: So I wonder.

00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:28.650
You have this other
question, sort

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:31.970
of the problem of the content.

00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:36.510
A family's willingness
to take on debt,

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:39.852
it's an evaluation of the
relative worth of the degree.

00:24:39.852 --> 00:24:41.310
RICHARD VEDDER:
Yeah, that's right.

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:42.185
MATTHEW MEHAN: Right.

00:24:42.185 --> 00:24:45.450
And I wonder if the problem
would be alleviated somewhat

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:48.510
if the American
people-- families,

00:24:48.510 --> 00:24:51.560
parents high school students--

00:24:51.560 --> 00:24:55.070
were better at appraising
the relative value.

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:56.820
RICHARD VEDDER: That is true.

00:24:56.820 --> 00:24:59.060
There's one thing--

00:24:59.060 --> 00:25:00.740
I've been condemning
the government.

00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:03.860
I suppose I should
be at least halfway

00:25:03.860 --> 00:25:07.885
nice to the government for
maybe seven or eight seconds.

00:25:11.170 --> 00:25:14.390
Today, the federal
government announced

00:25:14.390 --> 00:25:17.600
that they in their
college scorecard, which

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:21.560
is a document where you
can download information

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:28.610
college by college, they report
the alleged average earnings

00:25:28.610 --> 00:25:32.550
of graduates by college.

00:25:32.550 --> 00:25:36.360
And now they're giving you
the data even my major.

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:37.790
So you could look at--

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:41.330
you can't look at Hillsdale
because Hillsdale is verboten.

00:25:41.330 --> 00:25:43.310
They're out of the system.

00:25:43.310 --> 00:25:46.790
Hillsdale is not in because
they refuse to kowtow

00:25:46.790 --> 00:25:48.480
to the federal government.

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:51.270
But most schools you can look--

00:25:51.270 --> 00:25:56.780
for example, at my university
I looked up economics majors,

00:25:56.780 --> 00:25:59.600
and I learned that the average
earnings after graduation

00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:01.460
was $30,000.

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:06.230
And I said, my gosh, that
strikes me as awfully low.

00:26:06.230 --> 00:26:08.810
And I learned that the
average debt of students

00:26:08.810 --> 00:26:11.060
is $25,000 upon graduation.

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:14.970
That statistic is probably
fairly close to accurate.

00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:16.940
So that's a nice statistic.

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:19.520
And you could do this
for any college anywhere.

00:26:19.520 --> 00:26:24.350
The young lady who's sitting
in the fourth row from Yale

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:31.460
but who is still looking
for a job, that's

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:35.360
probably $72,000 at Yale.

00:26:35.360 --> 00:26:39.290
And their debt is less
because Yale drops money

00:26:39.290 --> 00:26:42.650
out airplanes over
their students

00:26:42.650 --> 00:26:47.480
because they got a $25,
$30 billion endowment.

00:26:47.480 --> 00:26:50.530
But you get that statistic,
and that's good information.

00:26:50.530 --> 00:26:52.445
So the federal government
has the potential

00:26:52.445 --> 00:26:54.080
of providing information.

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:58.790
Turns out, there's only 47 Ohio
University economics graduates

00:26:58.790 --> 00:26:59.892
in the sample.

00:26:59.892 --> 00:27:01.850
When there are hundreds
and thousands of these,

00:27:01.850 --> 00:27:04.430
only 47 made the sample.

00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:07.210
And then it turns out, oh,
you don't get in the sample

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:10.460
unless you had borrowed money
from the federal government

00:27:10.460 --> 00:27:11.670
to begin with.

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:14.973
Well, there are a lot of
people who don't borrow money

00:27:14.973 --> 00:27:16.140
from the federal government.

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:21.230
So the data is vastly distorted,
just horrendously distorted.

00:27:21.230 --> 00:27:22.490
But it's an attempt.

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:24.260
The attempt is a good one.

00:27:24.260 --> 00:27:26.240
And I've talked to
Secretary DeVos,

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:29.060
who I think is an
honorable person

00:27:29.060 --> 00:27:30.860
from the state of Michigan.

00:27:30.860 --> 00:27:36.220
I see the Van Andel name on
this very building, associates

00:27:36.220 --> 00:27:39.920
of the DeVos family from
Grand Rapids, Michigan.

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:42.620
Wonderful family,
wonderful people.

00:27:42.620 --> 00:27:46.430
But I love Betsy DeVos.

00:27:46.430 --> 00:27:49.280
But I wish they would
close her operation down,

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:53.630
and maybe it's time to let
the Georgetown University ROTC

00:27:53.630 --> 00:27:57.740
have a bombing practice over
Maryland Avenue in Washington,

00:27:57.740 --> 00:28:00.810
where the Department of
Education is located.

00:28:00.810 --> 00:28:01.510
Anyway--

00:28:01.510 --> 00:28:03.890
MATTHEW MEHAN: Again, he does
not speak for the college.

00:28:03.890 --> 00:28:06.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:07.540 --> 00:28:08.180
Yes.

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:13.100
So barring airstrikes,
the last time

00:28:13.100 --> 00:28:17.760
I heard a candidate
credibly call

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:21.165
for the destruction of the
Department of Ed was Bob Dole.

00:28:21.165 --> 00:28:22.040
RICHARD VEDDER: Yeah.

00:28:22.040 --> 00:28:24.040
MATTHEW MEHAN: It was
part of his loud platform,

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:26.643
was the big push.

00:28:26.643 --> 00:28:28.310
RICHARD VEDDER: How
old is Bob Dole now?

00:28:28.310 --> 00:28:29.640
142?

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:30.550
143?

00:28:30.550 --> 00:28:32.055
Yeah.

00:28:32.055 --> 00:28:34.430
MATTHEW MEHAN: One of the
things I found very interesting

00:28:34.430 --> 00:28:41.450
about your book is
you deeply ensconced

00:28:41.450 --> 00:28:43.640
in the economics of higher ed.

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:46.280
Who's paying for what?

00:28:46.280 --> 00:28:48.330
Where's the government funding?

00:28:48.330 --> 00:28:54.440
What state schools versus
the Ivy League or the plucky

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:59.150
liberal arts college or
the citadel of reason

00:28:59.150 --> 00:29:01.340
that is Hillsdale--

00:29:01.340 --> 00:29:02.645
who's paying for what?

00:29:02.645 --> 00:29:04.520
RICHARD VEDDER: Well,
that's a good question.

00:29:04.520 --> 00:29:07.550
I can't answer the
question definitively,

00:29:07.550 --> 00:29:10.730
but I would say that
there are a lot of people

00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:16.630
who are seeking educational
attainment of some sort who

00:29:16.630 --> 00:29:19.020
are really getting relatively
little from the federal

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:19.520
government.

00:29:19.520 --> 00:29:24.050
What about the person who
graduates from high school who

00:29:24.050 --> 00:29:28.370
is not academically terribly
interested in academic affairs

00:29:28.370 --> 00:29:31.250
and not very adept
but is pretty good

00:29:31.250 --> 00:29:35.120
with his hands or her
hands and wants to work

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:39.840
as a welder or a plumber?

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:42.090
It's going to take a
year or two of training

00:29:42.090 --> 00:29:43.830
to make that happen.

00:29:43.830 --> 00:29:50.640
Or take this very brainy,
high IQ kid who's good

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:53.790
at coding, whatever coding is--

00:29:53.790 --> 00:29:57.360
something to do vaguely
with computers, I'm told--

00:29:57.360 --> 00:30:02.130
and very good and they can
go to a coding academy,

00:30:02.130 --> 00:30:07.530
and in six months, if
they're good, learn a lot,

00:30:07.530 --> 00:30:11.760
and get a job paying $60,000
or $70,000 or $80,000 a year.

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:15.510
And the whole cost of the
coding academy experience

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:17.490
is $15,000 or $20,000.

00:30:17.490 --> 00:30:19.920
Those people are
getting almost nothing

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:23.260
from the federal
government, almost nothing.

00:30:23.260 --> 00:30:25.700
The kids who go-- and
I'm picking on Yale.

00:30:25.700 --> 00:30:28.760
Yale is a nice school.

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:33.780
Some would say it's the
best in the Ivy Leagues,

00:30:33.780 --> 00:30:38.250
and I sort of agree.

00:30:38.250 --> 00:30:39.660
I kind of like Yale.

00:30:39.660 --> 00:30:45.420
It's got that
yuppie, Harry Potter

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:48.840
look about it that's very nice.

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:50.910
I always thought
Harry Potter was

00:30:50.910 --> 00:30:53.370
one of the most positive
recent contributions

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:54.810
to Western civilization.

00:30:59.930 --> 00:31:02.930
Why are we giving
more to the kids that

00:31:02.930 --> 00:31:07.760
go to Yale who come from
relatively well-to-do families

00:31:07.760 --> 00:31:09.170
to begin with?

00:31:09.170 --> 00:31:11.050
And not only what we
give to the kids--

00:31:11.050 --> 00:31:12.800
MATTHEW MEHAN: But how
do we give to Yale?

00:31:12.800 --> 00:31:14.270
RICHARD VEDDER:
Well, that's right.

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:16.230
How do we give to Yale?

00:31:16.230 --> 00:31:20.400
I did a study once comparing
Princeton University--

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:24.030
the gal from Yale is having
heartburn back there,

00:31:24.030 --> 00:31:28.340
so I will now pick on Princeton
for a minute, if I may.

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:30.740
Here's two schools--
a tail of two schools.

00:31:30.740 --> 00:31:32.050
How far apart are they?

00:31:32.050 --> 00:31:33.320
11 miles.

00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:36.980
One of them is called Princeton
University, the other one,

00:31:36.980 --> 00:31:41.630
until recently, was called
Trenton State University.

00:31:41.630 --> 00:31:44.570
It is now called College
of New Jersey, which is

00:31:44.570 --> 00:31:46.190
the original name of Princeton.

00:31:46.190 --> 00:31:49.320
A brilliant marketing move
on their part, by the way.

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:51.260
Here are these two colleges.

00:31:51.260 --> 00:31:53.700
We call Princeton
a private school.

00:31:53.700 --> 00:31:57.350
We call the College of New
Jersey a public school.

00:31:57.350 --> 00:32:01.160
How much money does the public
school, College of New Jersey,

00:32:01.160 --> 00:32:02.960
get directly or indirectly?

00:32:02.960 --> 00:32:09.790
Direct government subsidies,
grants to students, research,

00:32:09.790 --> 00:32:10.900
whatever.

00:32:10.900 --> 00:32:13.810
Maybe $3,000, $4,000
or $5,000 per student.

00:32:13.810 --> 00:32:16.600
State of New Jersey adds
a couple thousand dollars.

00:32:16.600 --> 00:32:18.140
Maybe $6,000, $7,000.

00:32:18.140 --> 00:32:19.870
Let's exaggerate.

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:21.850
What does Princeton get?

00:32:21.850 --> 00:32:26.820
Princeton gets the
advantages of tax deductions

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:35.560
against an income, which fund
a ton of things, donations

00:32:35.560 --> 00:32:37.900
to buildings.

00:32:37.900 --> 00:32:41.380
For example, there is
Whitman College at Princeton.

00:32:41.380 --> 00:32:42.790
Any of you been to Princeton?

00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:47.170
Whitman College at Princeton,
lovely, lovely place.

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:52.360
Named after Meg
Whitman, a minor figure

00:32:52.360 --> 00:32:54.080
in American public finance--

00:32:54.080 --> 00:32:59.980
of eBay fame, et cetera, et
cetera, whatever else she did.

00:32:59.980 --> 00:33:03.310
She gave this little
college a little dorm

00:33:03.310 --> 00:33:05.230
for the kids at Princeton.

00:33:05.230 --> 00:33:07.780
350 kids live there.

00:33:07.780 --> 00:33:09.610
It's got stained glass windows.

00:33:09.610 --> 00:33:12.410
Ceiling in the dining
room is 34 feet high.

00:33:12.410 --> 00:33:14.823
I had it measured.

00:33:14.823 --> 00:33:16.240
I talked to people
in the-- what's

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:17.980
the newspaper at Princeton?

00:33:17.980 --> 00:33:19.760
A great student newspaper there.

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:22.510
They checked this out for me.

00:33:22.510 --> 00:33:29.050
The building cost $129 million
to build for 350 students.

00:33:29.050 --> 00:33:31.330
This was a decade ago.

00:33:31.330 --> 00:33:34.840
About $350,000 a student.

00:33:34.840 --> 00:33:38.710
Meg Whitman got
zillions of dollars--

00:33:38.710 --> 00:33:41.190
her tax burden was
reduced by millions.

00:33:41.190 --> 00:33:44.020
And I have nothing
against Meg Whitman,

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:46.990
I have nothing
against Princeton.

00:33:46.990 --> 00:33:50.230
So we gave a lot of
benefit to these people.

00:33:50.230 --> 00:33:52.990
If you were a progressive
liberal Democrat,

00:33:52.990 --> 00:33:55.600
I think the best
stand to take today

00:33:55.600 --> 00:34:00.910
to be different than the people
that are running is to say,

00:34:00.910 --> 00:34:03.100
I want to help the
poor, I want to do away

00:34:03.100 --> 00:34:05.710
with federal programs,
I want to do away

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:07.750
with these programs that
are helping the rich,

00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:10.219
the people that go to
The Heights School--

00:34:10.219 --> 00:34:11.420
this is where he teaches.

00:34:11.420 --> 00:34:13.530
I'm really being
mean to you now.

00:34:13.530 --> 00:34:16.030
MATTHEW MEHAN: Any institutions
that anyone's involved with,

00:34:16.030 --> 00:34:17.050
just make fun of it.

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:17.925
RICHARD VEDDER: Yeah.

00:34:17.925 --> 00:34:18.699
Yeah.

00:34:18.699 --> 00:34:19.840
No, I'm sorry.

00:34:19.840 --> 00:34:20.380
I shouldn't.

00:34:20.380 --> 00:34:21.643
That was beyond the pale.

00:34:21.643 --> 00:34:22.810
I should not have said that.

00:34:26.239 --> 00:34:28.870
And that's why I love Hillsdale.

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:31.355
I really do because
it has got principles.

00:34:31.355 --> 00:34:32.980
MATTHEW MEHAN: But
again, what would be

00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:36.489
the solution to this problem?

00:34:36.489 --> 00:34:38.560
Is it an education problem?

00:34:38.560 --> 00:34:40.150
People need to understand--

00:34:40.150 --> 00:34:43.330
RICHARD VEDDER: Well,
there are ideas out there.

00:34:43.330 --> 00:34:46.929
We're not going to change this
overnight, let's accept that.

00:34:46.929 --> 00:34:48.790
So if you want to be
practical-- and let's

00:34:48.790 --> 00:34:53.830
be politically
reasonable for a second.

00:34:53.830 --> 00:35:01.140
There are some ideas out there
that would get at the problem

00:35:01.140 --> 00:35:03.370
and start to do
something about it.

00:35:03.370 --> 00:35:05.610
Do we really need to
give student loans

00:35:05.610 --> 00:35:08.640
to people who are
in graduate school

00:35:08.640 --> 00:35:12.810
for their eighth or ninth year
and getting an MBA at Harvard?

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:15.300
Could we put some limits on
the student loan program?

00:35:15.300 --> 00:35:19.050
That's one approach,
put limits on.

00:35:19.050 --> 00:35:25.070
Could we say--
let's make it clear

00:35:25.070 --> 00:35:32.360
that people can go to college
and sell equity in themselves

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:35.160
rather than debt in themselves.

00:35:35.160 --> 00:35:38.960
When a person goes to
college and takes out a loan,

00:35:38.960 --> 00:35:41.780
they are, in effect--

00:35:41.780 --> 00:35:44.150
there's an IOU involved here.

00:35:44.150 --> 00:35:49.070
I owe you, the federal
government, $20,000 $30,000.

00:35:49.070 --> 00:35:50.100
That's what it is.

00:35:50.100 --> 00:35:52.530
It's a debt instrument.

00:35:52.530 --> 00:35:56.930
Why don't they issue equity
interests in themselves?

00:35:56.930 --> 00:36:00.080
Why don't they sell
stock in themselves?

00:36:00.080 --> 00:36:04.160
I will give you 7% of my
earnings for the first five

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:10.460
years after I graduate if you
give me $50,000 for my college.

00:36:10.460 --> 00:36:15.950
Income share agreements,
ISAs they're called.

00:36:15.950 --> 00:36:17.420
They're not illegal.

00:36:17.420 --> 00:36:20.100
But there is some
doubt in the law

00:36:20.100 --> 00:36:22.760
whether they're
enforceable contracts.

00:36:22.760 --> 00:36:26.820
And there are some who say, oh,
this is indentured servitude,

00:36:26.820 --> 00:36:31.260
and they come up with purple
language to describe them.

00:36:31.260 --> 00:36:33.920
I think they're perfectly
legitimate contractual

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:34.940
arrangements.

00:36:34.940 --> 00:36:36.500
So there are some
things we could

00:36:36.500 --> 00:36:40.130
do that would wean us away
from the student loan program

00:36:40.130 --> 00:36:44.120
and involve the private
sector and, incidentally, get

00:36:44.120 --> 00:36:45.620
the government out
of the business.

00:36:45.620 --> 00:36:46.870
Because this can all be done--

00:36:50.030 --> 00:36:55.340
JP Morgan Chase or Bank
of America or anyone

00:36:55.340 --> 00:36:57.800
could do this, Rocket--

00:36:57.800 --> 00:36:58.860
not Rocket Loans.

00:36:58.860 --> 00:36:59.730
What is it called?

00:36:59.730 --> 00:37:03.320
Anyway-- Quicken
Loans, whatever.

00:37:03.320 --> 00:37:06.960
Someone could do this
in the private sector.

00:37:06.960 --> 00:37:11.112
So there are alternatives
to what we're doing.

00:37:11.112 --> 00:37:12.570
MATTHEW MEHAN: And
what about the--

00:37:12.570 --> 00:37:14.670
we sort of touched on
it a little with regard

00:37:14.670 --> 00:37:20.250
to people who are going
for non-higher ed routes

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:21.708
to their careers.

00:37:21.708 --> 00:37:23.250
And there's a lot
of talk about that,

00:37:23.250 --> 00:37:26.340
about coming up
with trade schools

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:29.100
and making sure that there's
not this notion that you're not

00:37:29.100 --> 00:37:32.520
a real person if you don't get
the four year degree marker.

00:37:35.110 --> 00:37:38.860
But what about their
careers afterward problem?

00:37:41.580 --> 00:37:44.520
We haven't talked enough about
the third leg of the stool

00:37:44.520 --> 00:37:48.540
in terms of part of
the higher ed crisis.

00:37:51.060 --> 00:37:54.480
And what can we do to
better the reporting

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:55.650
that you were talking about?

00:37:55.650 --> 00:37:58.830
RICHARD VEDDER: Well,
taking the first thing is--

00:37:58.830 --> 00:38:04.110
I hate to say this in an
audience of college students

00:38:04.110 --> 00:38:08.400
and college graduates, but
I think there are two--

00:38:08.400 --> 00:38:13.090
we've overinvested in
higher ed in one sense.

00:38:13.090 --> 00:38:16.530
We put too much money into
traditional four year degree

00:38:16.530 --> 00:38:21.420
programs and too much
money into people

00:38:21.420 --> 00:38:25.110
getting a diploma for the
sake of getting diploma.

00:38:25.110 --> 00:38:29.430
And the government plays
a little role in this.

00:38:29.430 --> 00:38:33.690
President Obama promised
we would have the highest

00:38:33.690 --> 00:38:37.740
percentage of graduates.

00:38:37.740 --> 00:38:40.080
We were slipping
behind other nations,

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:42.120
and we'll get back
to number one.

00:38:42.120 --> 00:38:43.620
There are foundations
out there--

00:38:43.620 --> 00:38:48.840
the Lumina Foundation from
Indianapolis is one and other

00:38:48.840 --> 00:38:53.820
foundations who say, our
goal-- the Gates Foundation--

00:38:53.820 --> 00:38:58.350
is to increase the percentage
of Americans with degrees.

00:38:58.350 --> 00:39:01.740
So there is a mindless
push for people

00:39:01.740 --> 00:39:03.180
to get four year degrees.

00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:04.980
And we probably
ought to emphasize

00:39:04.980 --> 00:39:10.170
more these other
alternatives than we have.

00:39:10.170 --> 00:39:13.140
So that's part of what
we ought to be doing,

00:39:13.140 --> 00:39:17.020
is putting more emphasis
on these other things.

00:39:17.020 --> 00:39:19.860
And I think the
federal government is,

00:39:19.860 --> 00:39:23.760
as I indicated earlier, a
large part of the problem,

00:39:23.760 --> 00:39:27.180
but state governments
have contributed

00:39:27.180 --> 00:39:29.430
to this, too, and others.

00:39:29.430 --> 00:39:35.600
And well-meaning private people
have contributed to this.

00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:38.490
I think the government ought
to get out of the higher ed

00:39:38.490 --> 00:39:44.642
business in a perfect world.

00:39:44.642 --> 00:39:46.100
But I'll accept
the fact that we're

00:39:46.100 --> 00:39:48.740
going to have government
in the higher ed business

00:39:48.740 --> 00:39:52.350
and say, how can we reform it?

00:39:52.350 --> 00:39:55.430
And one way is to
privatize some parts of it,

00:39:55.430 --> 00:39:59.120
like the lending
process, to reduce

00:39:59.120 --> 00:40:08.480
the advantages associated with
this $130 million dormitories,

00:40:08.480 --> 00:40:13.550
and you give them money
to a stadium project,

00:40:13.550 --> 00:40:18.650
you get a tax deduction for
that so you can watch people

00:40:18.650 --> 00:40:21.610
throw balls back and forth.

00:40:21.610 --> 00:40:26.990
What's the public policy
reason why we subsidize

00:40:26.990 --> 00:40:28.980
people throwing balls?

00:40:28.980 --> 00:40:30.650
I have nothing against
throwing balls.

00:40:30.650 --> 00:40:33.920
I kind of like to watch
football games once in a while,

00:40:33.920 --> 00:40:36.500
basketball games even more.

00:40:36.500 --> 00:40:39.950
But why do we
subsidize this stuff?

00:40:39.950 --> 00:40:41.120
Why?

00:40:41.120 --> 00:40:46.190
It makes zero sense
whatsoever, in my opinion.

00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:47.690
MATTHEW MEHAN: Isn't
there something

00:40:47.690 --> 00:41:00.600
on the moral plane of a basic
re-education about comfort?

00:41:00.600 --> 00:41:04.658
When I think of my own-- and I
was in a dinosaur environment

00:41:04.658 --> 00:41:06.075
that hadn't gotten
with the times.

00:41:06.075 --> 00:41:08.100
It was sleepy and small.

00:41:08.100 --> 00:41:12.700
My undergrad was one of
the last lousy cafeterias.

00:41:12.700 --> 00:41:15.200
And I was going to
school in the '90s.

00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:19.980
And then I went back and visited
there later on as an alum,

00:41:19.980 --> 00:41:25.110
and it had become unbelievably
luxurious, just beautiful,

00:41:25.110 --> 00:41:29.320
with 50 different parfait
stations and things like that.

00:41:29.320 --> 00:41:31.500
And it was still
nothing compared to most

00:41:31.500 --> 00:41:32.610
of the mainstream schools.

00:41:32.610 --> 00:41:35.930
RICHARD VEDDER: No, that's
one of the problems.

00:41:35.930 --> 00:41:38.670
There's-- I called it once in
a Wall Street Journal piece I

00:41:38.670 --> 00:41:41.820
wrote the country
clubization of higher ed.

00:41:41.820 --> 00:41:43.720
And there is that.

00:41:43.720 --> 00:41:45.450
You've got to have--

00:41:45.450 --> 00:41:52.080
these kids, they're
living in absolute poverty

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:56.400
because we don't have a
lazy river at our school.

00:41:56.400 --> 00:41:58.400
You've got to have a lazy
river at your school.

00:41:58.400 --> 00:42:00.090
MATTHEW MEHAN: You have to
explain what a lazy river is.

00:42:00.090 --> 00:42:01.715
RICHARD VEDDER: A
lazy river is what we

00:42:01.715 --> 00:42:03.180
used to call a swimming pool.

00:42:03.180 --> 00:42:07.050
But instead of being 100 feet
long, it's 1,000 feet long,

00:42:07.050 --> 00:42:12.120
and you can rent a little raft.

00:42:12.120 --> 00:42:15.520
And if it's at a progressive
school that most of these are,

00:42:15.520 --> 00:42:17.130
it has a little
place on the raft--

00:42:17.130 --> 00:42:20.340
I've seen some of these,
an inner tube where there's

00:42:20.340 --> 00:42:24.930
room for a cup to put your beer
in while you go down the river

00:42:24.930 --> 00:42:28.260
and contemplate life
and get stoned or drunk,

00:42:28.260 --> 00:42:31.980
or whatever kids do these days.

00:42:31.980 --> 00:42:35.750
Why the heck should we
subsidize this kind of stuff?

00:42:35.750 --> 00:42:40.630
So there is a lot of that
and fancy dorms and so on.

00:42:40.630 --> 00:42:41.410
That's part of it.

00:42:41.410 --> 00:42:45.300
Another manifestation of the
rising costs of higher ed

00:42:45.300 --> 00:42:48.420
is an area I already mentioned,
administrative bloat.

00:42:48.420 --> 00:42:52.320
And it's bad on a cost basis.

00:42:52.320 --> 00:42:54.210
It costs a lot of money.

00:42:54.210 --> 00:42:56.850
But what's worse is
it's crowding out

00:42:56.850 --> 00:43:00.180
the academic function
of the university.

00:43:00.180 --> 00:43:05.780
The academic function
is getting downgraded

00:43:05.780 --> 00:43:11.630
by people who are functionaries,
I think the French might call

00:43:11.630 --> 00:43:17.360
them, people who are doing
things that have nothing

00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:20.810
to do with learning but a
lot to do with other things.

00:43:20.810 --> 00:43:24.380
I mentioned diversity and
inclusion as an example,

00:43:24.380 --> 00:43:27.380
but I could have
mentioned even fundraising

00:43:27.380 --> 00:43:31.670
as an example,
where universities

00:43:31.670 --> 00:43:33.920
are just overwhelmed
with administrators.

00:43:33.920 --> 00:43:36.830
And there's a lot of
evidence for this.

00:43:36.830 --> 00:43:40.690
Up the road, the greatest
argument for this

00:43:40.690 --> 00:43:46.190
is a guy up at Hopkins in
Baltimore, Ben Ginsberg.

00:43:46.190 --> 00:43:47.930
A great guy, Benjamin Ginsberg.

00:43:47.930 --> 00:43:49.780
He's a professor of
political science.

00:43:49.780 --> 00:43:56.030
He's probably politically left
of Elizabeth Warren for all

00:43:56.030 --> 00:44:00.410
I know, but he says the
universities are being

00:44:00.410 --> 00:44:02.360
destroyed by administrators.

00:44:02.360 --> 00:44:04.280
We no longer make the decisions.

00:44:04.280 --> 00:44:06.590
We're no longer in charge.

00:44:06.590 --> 00:44:09.320
And whether the faculty
ought to be in charge

00:44:09.320 --> 00:44:10.710
is a debatable issue.

00:44:10.710 --> 00:44:14.230
I'm not sure they should
be totally in charge.

00:44:14.230 --> 00:44:17.330
I am not sure I'd buy a used
car from the average faculty

00:44:17.330 --> 00:44:18.630
member.

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:25.760
But they are doing what
universities are supposed

00:44:25.760 --> 00:44:29.480
to do, they're creating
and disseminating

00:44:29.480 --> 00:44:34.370
knowledge and ideals and virtue.

00:44:34.370 --> 00:44:37.505
Virtue, by the way, has gone
by the wayside at most schools.

00:44:40.070 --> 00:44:43.030
There are some exceptions,
but most schools.

00:44:43.030 --> 00:44:44.485
We don't care about virtue.

00:44:44.485 --> 00:44:45.860
MATTHEW MEHAN: Or
they've decided

00:44:45.860 --> 00:44:48.195
to try a new idea
of what virtue is.

00:44:48.195 --> 00:44:49.070
RICHARD VEDDER: Yeah.

00:44:49.070 --> 00:44:51.710
MATTHEW MEHAN: Vigorously
trying to train everyone

00:44:51.710 --> 00:44:53.985
in virtues that may not
actually be virtues.

00:44:53.985 --> 00:44:54.860
RICHARD VEDDER: Yeah.

00:44:54.860 --> 00:45:02.180
I think virtues that in the
sweep of Western civilization

00:45:02.180 --> 00:45:05.107
over the last several
thousand years

00:45:05.107 --> 00:45:06.440
would not be considered virtues.

00:45:08.787 --> 00:45:10.370
MATTHEW MEHAN: So
before we open it up

00:45:10.370 --> 00:45:14.660
to questions from the
audience-- and when we do,

00:45:14.660 --> 00:45:17.360
we'll have microphones for you.

00:45:17.360 --> 00:45:21.800
So just raise your hand and
I'll call on you one at a time.

00:45:21.800 --> 00:45:25.460
I wanted to ask you to--

00:45:25.460 --> 00:45:26.665
and this is Washington.

00:45:26.665 --> 00:45:29.030
A lot of people
who actually really

00:45:29.030 --> 00:45:33.410
care about trying to make the
doable change that touch off

00:45:33.410 --> 00:45:35.410
the first snowflakes at
the top of the mountain,

00:45:35.410 --> 00:45:37.790
they could start an
avalanche and actually get

00:45:37.790 --> 00:45:43.920
something done to solve big
problems with little changes.

00:45:43.920 --> 00:45:46.550
If you were talking to a
room of interested people

00:45:46.550 --> 00:45:49.610
like this and donors
and politicians

00:45:49.610 --> 00:45:52.280
and said, what are we all
going to rally around,

00:45:52.280 --> 00:45:54.290
where do we put the
very tip of the spear

00:45:54.290 --> 00:45:55.940
in, all of our
weight, what would you

00:45:55.940 --> 00:45:58.970
choose as the one
thing to push on?

00:45:58.970 --> 00:46:01.365
I know usually, solutions
are multi-valent

00:46:01.365 --> 00:46:02.852
and you've got to do a lot.

00:46:02.852 --> 00:46:05.060
But if you had to pick one,
what would it be and why?

00:46:05.060 --> 00:46:10.890
RICHARD VEDDER: Well,
you're being nasty to me.

00:46:10.890 --> 00:46:13.650
You're expecting me--

00:46:13.650 --> 00:46:19.740
I'm good at criticizing
people, but solutions?

00:46:19.740 --> 00:46:25.890
I'm a lowly college professor
in Appalachia eking out

00:46:25.890 --> 00:46:30.840
a bare existence teaching.

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:35.580
As I said to begin with,
in a practical sense

00:46:35.580 --> 00:46:40.140
and in a Washingtonian
sense looking at Washington,

00:46:40.140 --> 00:46:43.380
I suspect the single
most important thing

00:46:43.380 --> 00:46:49.980
that we could do would be to
work to downsize and change

00:46:49.980 --> 00:46:54.450
the student loan program, which
would help with the cost side.

00:46:54.450 --> 00:46:56.880
And the money that
those programs

00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:59.250
raise through
higher tuition fees

00:46:59.250 --> 00:47:03.060
pay for all these
administrators and lazy rivers

00:47:03.060 --> 00:47:07.320
and a $17 million
football coach.

00:47:07.320 --> 00:47:10.080
Well, they don't pay for
the football coaches.

00:47:10.080 --> 00:47:12.450
Football coaches, by the
way, pay for themselves

00:47:12.450 --> 00:47:15.480
at the really good schools.

00:47:15.480 --> 00:47:18.390
I'm not saying
they're all virtuous.

00:47:18.390 --> 00:47:19.290
Au contraire.

00:47:21.960 --> 00:47:23.380
But that's what I would do.

00:47:23.380 --> 00:47:28.110
I would work on getting
the government out

00:47:28.110 --> 00:47:32.590
of the business of doing the
harm that they do the most.

00:47:32.590 --> 00:47:36.780
And the most single harm
they do is in this area.

00:47:36.780 --> 00:47:38.422
So can we let it go at that?

00:47:38.422 --> 00:47:39.630
MATTHEW MEHAN: We'll have to.

00:47:39.630 --> 00:47:40.610
Very good.

00:47:40.610 --> 00:47:41.820
So questions?

00:47:41.820 --> 00:47:44.940
Yes, please.

00:47:44.940 --> 00:47:47.100
AUDIENCE: Thank you
for a fascinating talk.

00:47:47.100 --> 00:47:51.000
I wanted to ask if this problem
that you've outlined here

00:47:51.000 --> 00:47:54.000
isn't something that's
common in human affairs.

00:47:54.000 --> 00:47:56.070
It's a lack of good governance.

00:47:56.070 --> 00:47:58.860
And I'm wondering what
sort of structures

00:47:58.860 --> 00:48:03.570
academic institutions could
adopt that would improve that.

00:48:03.570 --> 00:48:06.270
In this very room
of a few weeks ago,

00:48:06.270 --> 00:48:11.040
there was a presentation about
the problem at Oberlin College,

00:48:11.040 --> 00:48:15.570
and it really sounds outrageous.

00:48:15.570 --> 00:48:19.230
You can look at the corporate
world and look at the way

00:48:19.230 --> 00:48:24.120
a corporate board of directors
works, but it seems to me

00:48:24.120 --> 00:48:24.972
that alumni--

00:48:24.972 --> 00:48:26.430
and people talk
about stockholders.

00:48:26.430 --> 00:48:31.170
When you graduate, if you become
a shareholder in the university

00:48:31.170 --> 00:48:33.180
or so, you have
a vested interest

00:48:33.180 --> 00:48:37.440
in protecting the reputation
of your sheepskin or whatever.

00:48:37.440 --> 00:48:39.390
It seems to me that
there are a lot of ways

00:48:39.390 --> 00:48:40.540
to approach this problem.

00:48:40.540 --> 00:48:41.873
Do you have any comment on that?

00:48:41.873 --> 00:48:45.120
RICHARD VEDDER: Well, I think
university governance is

00:48:45.120 --> 00:48:49.590
horrible the way it happens,
and that gets to your question.

00:48:49.590 --> 00:48:52.770
Boards of trustees
at universities

00:48:52.770 --> 00:48:55.770
are, I think, mostly
well-intended people

00:48:55.770 --> 00:48:59.620
or truly interested in
their college and so forth.

00:48:59.620 --> 00:49:04.770
But they're clueless about
2/3 of the bad things

00:49:04.770 --> 00:49:07.000
that happen on campus.

00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:11.830
I don't know the specifics of
the Oberlin College details,

00:49:11.830 --> 00:49:14.550
but I'm sure that
the board of trustees

00:49:14.550 --> 00:49:17.280
at Oberlin College, a
large majority of them

00:49:17.280 --> 00:49:19.770
didn't have a clue
what was going

00:49:19.770 --> 00:49:23.430
on at Gibson's
Bakery in Oberlin,

00:49:23.430 --> 00:49:27.825
Ohio that has led to
embarrassment to Oberlin

00:49:27.825 --> 00:49:30.990
and tens of millions of
dollars of potential damage

00:49:30.990 --> 00:49:35.970
for them, hurt the college
greatly, led to some absolutely

00:49:35.970 --> 00:49:40.050
grotesque behavior on the part
of administrators who should

00:49:40.050 --> 00:49:42.910
have been, frankly, just fired.

00:49:42.910 --> 00:49:45.720
And so in my--

00:49:45.720 --> 00:49:48.120
I don't want to
keep trying to sell

00:49:48.120 --> 00:49:51.750
my book because both people
that read it have liked it.

00:49:55.082 --> 00:49:56.790
MATTHEW MEHAN: We will
be selling books--

00:49:56.790 --> 00:49:58.457
RICHARD VEDDER: We
will be selling books

00:49:58.457 --> 00:50:01.420
at a fairly reasonable price.

00:50:01.420 --> 00:50:05.700
And I will leave an autograph
in them if you want,

00:50:05.700 --> 00:50:06.960
which I doubt you will.

00:50:06.960 --> 00:50:16.710
But there is a governance
issue in higher ed.

00:50:16.710 --> 00:50:19.560
Who owns the university?

00:50:19.560 --> 00:50:21.660
Who runs the universities?

00:50:21.660 --> 00:50:23.550
Well, the faculty think they do.

00:50:23.550 --> 00:50:25.610
They don't usually,
but they think they do.

00:50:25.610 --> 00:50:27.990
And at some schools,
they have a lot of power.

00:50:27.990 --> 00:50:30.510
Larry Summers, who is president
of Harvard University,

00:50:30.510 --> 00:50:35.520
was Secretary of the Treasury,
he is Mr. Everything,

00:50:35.520 --> 00:50:36.390
he got fired.

00:50:36.390 --> 00:50:38.160
Who fired him?

00:50:38.160 --> 00:50:40.810
The faculty.

00:50:40.810 --> 00:50:44.610
He didn't like the notion that
women were as good at math

00:50:44.610 --> 00:50:46.060
as men or something.

00:50:46.060 --> 00:50:49.360
So that's a basis
for firing, I guess.

00:50:49.360 --> 00:50:55.810
So the faculty don't
control the university.

00:50:55.810 --> 00:50:58.195
The alumni think they
control the university.

00:50:58.195 --> 00:50:59.360
They give a lot of money.

00:50:59.360 --> 00:51:02.950
At some schools they have
some clout, but at most places

00:51:02.950 --> 00:51:05.590
they don't own the university.

00:51:05.590 --> 00:51:08.633
The administration
probably has more claim

00:51:08.633 --> 00:51:09.550
to own the university.

00:51:09.550 --> 00:51:11.080
Who owns the universities?

00:51:11.080 --> 00:51:12.610
No one knows.

00:51:12.610 --> 00:51:15.810
Well, theoretically, it's the
board of trustees or the board

00:51:15.810 --> 00:51:16.390
or governor.

00:51:16.390 --> 00:51:20.490
In Virginia, it's the
board of visitors.

00:51:20.490 --> 00:51:22.140
Who's on the board of visitors?

00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:25.120
Someone the governor appoints
for a few years or someone,

00:51:25.120 --> 00:51:28.870
in the case of private schools,
that's given a lot of money,

00:51:28.870 --> 00:51:31.680
wants the prestige of the
name, goes to a meeting

00:51:31.680 --> 00:51:32.995
about four times a year.

00:51:32.995 --> 00:51:35.370
The president of the university
fills them with good wine

00:51:35.370 --> 00:51:38.940
and drinks, gives them
tickets to the football games

00:51:38.940 --> 00:51:44.340
and listens to the propaganda
that they feed the board, which

00:51:44.340 --> 00:51:47.790
bares modest or no
resemblance to what's

00:51:47.790 --> 00:51:49.930
really going on at the school.

00:51:49.930 --> 00:51:52.600
And this is shameful.

00:51:52.600 --> 00:51:55.080
But do I have an
easy solution for it?

00:51:55.080 --> 00:51:56.790
No, but I think
that's one area I

00:51:56.790 --> 00:51:59.142
would start looking
for some solutions.

00:51:59.142 --> 00:52:00.600
MATTHEW MEHAN: It
sounds like you'd

00:52:00.600 --> 00:52:03.535
want to get into the ear of a
governor and get them to take--

00:52:03.535 --> 00:52:04.410
RICHARD VEDDER: Yeah.

00:52:04.410 --> 00:52:07.620
By the way, I spent a year
on the Spellings Commission

00:52:07.620 --> 00:52:10.410
on the Future of
Higher Education,

00:52:10.410 --> 00:52:14.050
where I was supposed to
be part of a group of--

00:52:14.050 --> 00:52:15.900
a blue ribbon
group, and it really

00:52:15.900 --> 00:52:21.360
was a blue ribbon group of very
famous people, former cabinet

00:52:21.360 --> 00:52:26.690
members, governors,
presidents of universities,

00:52:26.690 --> 00:52:31.500
universities like Michigan
and MIT and so forth.

00:52:31.500 --> 00:52:33.900
I was on this really
blue ribbon group.

00:52:33.900 --> 00:52:36.210
Boy, we were going to
turn education around.

00:52:36.210 --> 00:52:36.990
What did we do?

00:52:36.990 --> 00:52:40.590
My wife was right from the
day once I agreed to be on it.

00:52:40.590 --> 00:52:44.030
She says, you're not
going to do a damn thing.

00:52:44.030 --> 00:52:46.620
She's more or less right.

00:52:46.620 --> 00:52:47.615
Anyway, sorry.

00:52:47.615 --> 00:52:49.990
MATTHEW MEHAN: Yes, there's
a question in the back there.

00:52:55.918 --> 00:52:56.460
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:52:56.460 --> 00:52:58.180
I'm one of the
Hillsdale students

00:52:58.180 --> 00:52:59.138
here on the DC program.

00:52:59.138 --> 00:53:00.222
RICHARD VEDDER: Wonderful.

00:53:00.222 --> 00:53:01.800
AUDIENCE: And at
Hillsdale, we really

00:53:01.800 --> 00:53:06.130
enjoy John Henry Newman and his
work, The Idea of a University.

00:53:06.130 --> 00:53:09.960
And in that, Newman talks about
how the biggest contribution

00:53:09.960 --> 00:53:12.390
a university makes
to the student

00:53:12.390 --> 00:53:15.450
is teaching him
or her how to look

00:53:15.450 --> 00:53:17.610
at the world as an
integrated whole, but also,

00:53:17.610 --> 00:53:20.600
the mark that the university
leaves on each student.

00:53:20.600 --> 00:53:26.250
And my question for you is,
how can we as college degree

00:53:26.250 --> 00:53:29.070
holders go out and teach
people about this idea

00:53:29.070 --> 00:53:29.940
and communicate it?

00:53:29.940 --> 00:53:31.860
Because this really does
seem like the promise

00:53:31.860 --> 00:53:34.800
of higher education
that's been lacking

00:53:34.800 --> 00:53:39.908
in this post-industrial
post-modern world.

00:53:39.908 --> 00:53:41.700
RICHARD VEDDER: It's
an excellent question.

00:53:41.700 --> 00:53:43.660
I missed a little bit
of the part of it,

00:53:43.660 --> 00:53:48.230
but I've read now Cardinal--

00:53:48.230 --> 00:53:53.180
now St. Newman-- he's
been promoted to a Saint.

00:53:53.180 --> 00:53:55.440
It's one of the
few areas of life

00:53:55.440 --> 00:53:58.980
where you can die and 100
years later get promoted up.

00:54:02.280 --> 00:54:09.420
The pope recently made
John Henry Newman a Saint.

00:54:09.420 --> 00:54:12.780
One of the greatest,
wonderful pieces of things

00:54:12.780 --> 00:54:13.620
I've ever read.

00:54:13.620 --> 00:54:15.390
I wish every student
read it, and I

00:54:15.390 --> 00:54:18.800
wish every member of the board
of trustees would read it.

00:54:21.480 --> 00:54:24.960
We've lost sight of
the big things in life

00:54:24.960 --> 00:54:27.600
and the important
things in life.

00:54:27.600 --> 00:54:28.470
We've lost sight.

00:54:28.470 --> 00:54:33.180
I just through the
word virtue out just

00:54:33.180 --> 00:54:36.420
as a shorthand
version of talking

00:54:36.420 --> 00:54:40.260
about the non-practical,
non-vocational dimensions

00:54:40.260 --> 00:54:41.820
of higher education.

00:54:41.820 --> 00:54:43.290
We've lost sight of them.

00:54:43.290 --> 00:54:48.490
But I think our nation has lost
sight of a lot of the things

00:54:48.490 --> 00:54:51.600
that used to be the glue
that brought us together

00:54:51.600 --> 00:54:52.790
as a nation.

00:54:52.790 --> 00:54:55.350
I'm getting a little
away from your question,

00:54:55.350 --> 00:54:58.140
but the glue that
makes us Americans

00:54:58.140 --> 00:55:00.690
is that we all have
some common thing

00:55:00.690 --> 00:55:04.320
that we hold in common
that we believe in,

00:55:04.320 --> 00:55:06.450
that we believe dear to us.

00:55:06.450 --> 00:55:07.200
We're losing that.

00:55:07.200 --> 00:55:10.110
First of all, kids aren't
even taking classes in history

00:55:10.110 --> 00:55:11.520
anymore at most schools.

00:55:11.520 --> 00:55:15.660
They don't know about
the Founding Fathers.

00:55:15.660 --> 00:55:17.310
And I think that's shameful.

00:55:17.310 --> 00:55:22.830
We need to move back to a core
curriculum, like Hillsdale

00:55:22.830 --> 00:55:23.430
has done.

00:55:23.430 --> 00:55:25.763
MATTHEW MEHAN: There's a group
of us wild-eyed reformers

00:55:25.763 --> 00:55:30.990
who are trying to remind the
liberal arts colleges in higher

00:55:30.990 --> 00:55:34.590
ed of their own tradition,
like to mistranslate

00:55:34.590 --> 00:55:38.100
or re-translate artes liberales,
where that phrase liberal

00:55:38.100 --> 00:55:43.680
arts came from, as the arts of
liberty just to shake it up.

00:55:43.680 --> 00:55:46.620
Part of what you're trying
to teach is self-government,

00:55:46.620 --> 00:55:51.100
and that's an interior thing
and an exterior political thing.

00:55:51.100 --> 00:55:52.770
And that's, I think, been lost.

00:55:52.770 --> 00:55:53.538
One more question.

00:55:53.538 --> 00:55:55.330
I think we have time
for one more question.

00:55:55.330 --> 00:55:56.050
RICHARD VEDDER:
Yes, in the back.

00:55:56.050 --> 00:55:58.740
MATTHEW MEHAN: The pink shirt
over there, or the blue jacket,

00:55:58.740 --> 00:55:59.662
whichever.

00:56:02.430 --> 00:56:05.130
AUDIENCE: So I work at a
university, Catholic U,

00:56:05.130 --> 00:56:07.540
and all the things
you said are true.

00:56:07.540 --> 00:56:08.040
I've seen--

00:56:08.040 --> 00:56:10.123
RICHARD VEDDER: Where
are you, Catholic U?

00:56:10.123 --> 00:56:10.790
AUDIENCE: Sorry?

00:56:10.790 --> 00:56:11.660
RICHARD VEDDER: What U?

00:56:11.660 --> 00:56:14.202
AUDIENCE: Catholic University
up the road, about three miles.

00:56:14.202 --> 00:56:17.227
You can see the dome--
the shrine from here.

00:56:17.227 --> 00:56:19.060
I was reading about
endowments the other day

00:56:19.060 --> 00:56:20.810
and the history of it,
and apparently they

00:56:20.810 --> 00:56:21.780
go back to Greece.

00:56:21.780 --> 00:56:25.335
There's [INAUDIBLE] Renaissance,
there's other nobles--

00:56:25.335 --> 00:56:27.337
I don't know-- of
France, the state--

00:56:27.337 --> 00:56:28.170
they were the state.

00:56:28.170 --> 00:56:31.740
The nobles of government
subsidized education.

00:56:31.740 --> 00:56:38.200
So it seems to me to be part of
the kingly power of a nation.

00:56:38.200 --> 00:56:40.770
It seems to me a
human nature thing.

00:56:40.770 --> 00:56:43.358
But you say we could do better.

00:56:43.358 --> 00:56:45.900
Endowments are different than--
what is your thinking about--

00:56:45.900 --> 00:56:47.192
this is already so much money--

00:56:47.192 --> 00:56:48.990
we've been hitting on Yale.

00:56:48.990 --> 00:56:51.750
Sorry, but there's
many endowments.

00:56:51.750 --> 00:56:53.153
I was googling the other day.

00:56:53.153 --> 00:56:53.820
There's so much.

00:56:53.820 --> 00:56:55.680
What is you thinking about--

00:56:55.680 --> 00:56:57.390
there's all that money
there that's in--

00:56:57.390 --> 00:57:00.630
you always get news, oh, Yale
is buying land in California

00:57:00.630 --> 00:57:02.365
with their endowment
to change the world.

00:57:02.365 --> 00:57:03.490
RICHARD VEDDER: Yeah, yeah.

00:57:03.490 --> 00:57:04.790
AUDIENCE: So sorry.

00:57:04.790 --> 00:57:05.610
That was long.

00:57:05.610 --> 00:57:08.130
RICHARD VEDDER:
Well, I did a piece--

00:57:08.130 --> 00:57:12.250
I did some serious empirical
work on endowments.

00:57:12.250 --> 00:57:17.560
First of all, Adam Smith had it
right in The Wealth of Nations,

00:57:17.560 --> 00:57:25.630
when he noted that the
professors at Oxford University

00:57:25.630 --> 00:57:31.120
no longer teach anything anymore
because the students are now--

00:57:31.120 --> 00:57:35.110
because in an earlier
period, the faculty

00:57:35.110 --> 00:57:37.700
got paid by the
students directly.

00:57:37.700 --> 00:57:40.480
And when that
stopped, the faculty

00:57:40.480 --> 00:57:43.270
didn't give a damn about
the students anymore.

00:57:43.270 --> 00:57:48.760
The world went to hell, which is
probably a slight exaggeration,

00:57:48.760 --> 00:57:50.200
but there is an
element of truth.

00:57:50.200 --> 00:57:53.770
And what happened is
endowments insulate us

00:57:53.770 --> 00:57:57.700
from having to pay any attention
to achieving excellence.

00:57:57.700 --> 00:57:59.800
Why should we?

00:57:59.800 --> 00:58:02.285
The money's coming in anyway.

00:58:02.285 --> 00:58:04.660
And endowment money,
by the way, mostly

00:58:04.660 --> 00:58:07.630
does not support lowering
costs to students.

00:58:07.630 --> 00:58:09.640
I've done empirical
work on this.

00:58:09.640 --> 00:58:11.860
I've run regression--
lots of economists,

00:58:11.860 --> 00:58:13.520
we run regression equations.

00:58:13.520 --> 00:58:15.940
I run tons of regression
equations on this.

00:58:15.940 --> 00:58:18.220
The money doesn't go
to support students.

00:58:18.220 --> 00:58:21.610
It does go to lower teaching
load for us faculty,

00:58:21.610 --> 00:58:24.400
it does go to
increase salaries, it

00:58:24.400 --> 00:58:29.250
does go to do a lot of
things that, really, I

00:58:29.250 --> 00:58:33.215
don't think justify
contribution to colleges.

00:58:33.215 --> 00:58:35.590
This is a terrible thing to
say here because I give money

00:58:35.590 --> 00:58:36.270
to colleges.

00:58:36.270 --> 00:58:40.290
I think colleges are
worthwhile endeavors.

00:58:40.290 --> 00:58:42.390
I love my alma mater.

00:58:42.390 --> 00:58:44.280
I love the school I teach at.

00:58:44.280 --> 00:58:45.660
I love Hillsdale.

00:58:45.660 --> 00:58:51.310
But some people have abused
that dimension of it as well.

00:58:51.310 --> 00:58:54.150
So I'm not against endowments.

00:58:54.150 --> 00:58:56.730
If I were, they will
not broadcast this

00:58:56.730 --> 00:59:00.140
to the rest of the world because
Larry Arnn, who runs Hillsdale,

00:59:00.140 --> 00:59:02.250
loves endowments,
and he should--

00:59:02.250 --> 00:59:02.760
he should.

00:59:02.760 --> 00:59:04.390
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:04.390 --> 00:59:09.570
But in any case,
endowments are an issue

00:59:09.570 --> 00:59:12.100
that need to be addressed.

00:59:12.100 --> 00:59:14.130
AUDIENCE: Why aren't
they forced to use those

00:59:14.130 --> 00:59:17.060
to lower the cost to
these attending schools,

00:59:17.060 --> 00:59:18.892
when they've got $3
billion sitting there?

00:59:18.892 --> 00:59:20.850
RICHARD VEDDER: Well,
there is an issue of what

00:59:20.850 --> 00:59:22.142
should be done with endowments.

00:59:22.142 --> 00:59:23.933
MATTHEW MEHAN: The
question was, why aren't

00:59:23.933 --> 00:59:25.260
they forced to use them to--

00:59:25.260 --> 00:59:27.090
RICHARD VEDDER: To lower costs.

00:59:27.090 --> 00:59:29.640
Well, that is a good
empirical question.

00:59:29.640 --> 00:59:34.830
I will say that when they put an
endowment tax in on very, very

00:59:34.830 --> 00:59:36.210
large endowments--

00:59:36.210 --> 00:59:40.380
I testified before the House of
Ways and Means Committee down

00:59:40.380 --> 00:59:43.410
the road here before
the Gang of 535,

00:59:43.410 --> 00:59:47.700
and I said there
is a case for them.

00:59:47.700 --> 00:59:51.840
And I usually don't promote
higher taxes on anything.

00:59:51.840 --> 00:59:56.910
But a tax on, I'll
say, Harvard, too--

00:59:56.910 --> 01:00:01.250
our young lady in the fourth row
is already suffering enough--

01:00:01.250 --> 01:00:05.250
a tax on Harvard probably
wouldn't hurt the world

01:00:05.250 --> 01:00:08.200
and might help it a little bit.

01:00:08.200 --> 01:00:10.700
MATTHEW MEHAN: Well, with that,
please join me in thank you.

01:00:10.700 --> 01:00:12.487
Thank you, Dr. Vedder.

01:00:12.487 --> 01:00:13.570
RICHARD VEDDER: Thank you.

01:00:13.570 --> 01:00:16.920
[APPLAUSE]

